-- title:
--   Debian's libsodium 1.0.22-2 (arm64) crypto_onetimeauth object code, as a placed library
-- statement:
--   The object code of libsodium's public one-time-authentication functions as Debian ships it for 64-bit ARM, as one placed library in the `Arm64` semantics (google-deepmind/code-verification's `Arm64.Trusted`, republished as the `Def_Arm64_*` bundles). The three objects are `libsodium_la-crypto_onetimeauth.o`, `libsodium_la-onetimeauth_poly1305.o` and `libsodium_la-poly1305_donna.o`, taken unmodified from `/usr/lib/aarch64-linux-gnu/libsodium.a` in the Debian package `libsodium-dev_1.0.22-2_arm64.deb` (libsodium 1.0.22). Debian compiled them from `src/libsodium/crypto_onetimeauth/crypto_onetimeauth.c` (the generic `crypto_onetimeauth*` API), `src/libsodium/crypto_onetimeauth/poly1305/onetimeauth_poly1305.c` (the `crypto_onetimeauth_poly1305*` functions, which call the implementation selected at startup through a pointer to a table of functions) and `src/libsodium/crypto_onetimeauth/poly1305/donna/poly1305_donna.c` (the portable implementation, with `poly1305_donna64.h`: three limbs of 44, 44 and 42 bits and 128-bit products).
--
--   **Provenance.** The package's SHA-256 is `a78d13b5…8f3f`, as listed in Debian's `Packages` index for arm64 in unstable and testing, whose hash is in the `InRelease` files signed with Debian's archive keys. The objects' SHA-256s are `b75967a4…eda`, `c375c62d…4ee` and `3f97dc8c…2d5`. Every function of the three objects occurs byte for byte in the shared library `libsodium.so.26.4.0` of the package `libsodium26_1.0.22-2_arm64.deb`, apart from the words the linker fills in. `rust-obj2lean export` linked the three objects into this module. Its byte sidecar is inlined as literal lists, its imports are renamed to the published bundles, and its docstring's account of the upstream build is replaced (the file says so). DeepMind's exporter rejects ELF note sections, so we used a copy with a one-line change that skips them: [skip-note-sections.patch](https://github.com/dbenbenn/arm64-semantics-p2m/blob/159faf00becda47371f86336df32aa1c3e60bae4/tools/rust-obj2lean/skip-note-sections.patch), applied by the build script beside it. The objects' only note section is `.note.gnu.property`, which marks them as built with branch protection. No code reads it. The bundle's 1,800 code bytes are the three objects' `.text` sections, concatenated in order with their alignment padding.
--
--   **The library `libsodiumDebianOnetimeauthLib`.**
--   - `libsodiumDebianOnetimeauthText` is the code block: 1,800 bytes holding 28 function symbols with their offsets, the ten functions of `crypto_onetimeauth.c` from `0x0`, the ten of `onetimeauth_poly1305.c` from `0xa0`, and the eight of `poly1305_donna.c` from `0x1a0` (`poly1305_blocks`, `poly1305_update`, the donna `_init` and `_update`, `poly1305_finish`, the donna `_final`, the one-shot and `_verify`; each name carries the suffix “ [libsodium_la_poly1305_donna]”). Gaps between the objects' sections are zero-filled.
--   - Each `crypto_onetimeauth*` function branches to its `crypto_onetimeauth_poly1305*` counterpart. Each of those loads the pointer `implementation` and, through it, an entry of the table `crypto_onetimeauth_poly1305_donna_implementation`, and branches there.
--   - The external symbols are `__stack_chk_fail`, `crypto_verify_16`, `memset`, `randombytes_buf` and `sodium_memzero`; the code also reads `__stack_chk_guard` through the global offset table. In the donna code, `poly1305_finish` calls `memset` and ends with a branch to `sodium_memzero`, the one-shot and `_verify` check the stack guard and call `__stack_chk_fail` if it changed, and `_verify` calls `crypto_verify_16`. The key-generation functions branch to `randombytes_buf`.
--   - There are three data objects: the 8-byte writable pointer `implementation` (an `abs64` relocation to the table), the 40-byte writable table (five `abs64` relocations to the donna one-shot, `_verify`, `_init`, `_update` and `_final`), and the read-only string `"poly1305"` with its terminating zero.
--   - The exports are `crypto_onetimeauth`, `crypto_onetimeauth_init`, `crypto_onetimeauth_update` and `crypto_onetimeauth_final`: the functions the mission's goal is about. The other public functions are in the block but not listed.
--   - Debian builds with branch protection. Most functions begin with a `BTI` or `PACIASP` instruction, and those that save the link register authenticate it with `AUTIASP` before returning. These are hint instructions, and the `Arm64` model executes every hint instruction as a no-op.
--
--   **The source of the dispatch.** `onetimeauth_poly1305.c`, lines 14–15 and 21: “static const crypto_onetimeauth_poly1305_implementation *implementation = &crypto_onetimeauth_poly1305_donna_implementation;”, “return implementation->onetimeauth(out, in, inlen, k);”; lines 37, 45 and 52 do the same for `onetimeauth_init`, `onetimeauth_update` and `onetimeauth_final`. `poly1305_donna.c`, lines 115–124, define the table: “struct crypto_onetimeauth_poly1305_implementation crypto_onetimeauth_poly1305_donna_implementation = { SODIUM_C99(.onetimeauth =) crypto_onetimeauth_poly1305_donna, SODIUM_C99(.onetimeauth_verify =) crypto_onetimeauth_poly1305_donna_verify, SODIUM_C99(.onetimeauth_init =) crypto_onetimeauth_poly1305_donna_init, SODIUM_C99(.onetimeauth_update =) crypto_onetimeauth_poly1305_donna_update, SODIUM_C99(.onetimeauth_final =) crypto_onetimeauth_poly1305_donna_final };”.
--
--   The statements quantify over every placement of this library that the `Arm64` model accepts (`Obj.PlacedLib.Placeable`), so no load address is fixed.
